California and Federal Legal Frameworks Governing AI and Robotics in Oncology Clinical Trials
Executive summary
Artificial intelligence (AI) and physical AI/robotics appear in clinical trials in at least three legally distinct roles: as the investigational product itself (e.g., an AI-enabled diagnostic algorithm or a robotic surgical system under study); as a device or software function used to collect trial data (e.g., imaging AI, digital pathology AI, wearables, robotics-assisted procedures); or as operational tooling used to run the trial (e.g., recruitment matching, eConsent/EDC automation, statistical analysis). The applicable legal regime depends primarily on intended use and risk, not on whether the technology is “AI” in a colloquial sense. [1]
At the federal level, the core binding obligations for AI/robotics in clinical trials typically come from: (i) FDA’s investigational product frameworks for drugs/biologics (IND, 21 CFR Part 312) and devices (IDE, 21 CFR Part 812), including cross-cutting human subject protection rules (21 CFR Parts 50 and 56); (ii) the Common Rule (45 CFR Part 46) when applicable (often through funding or institutional policy); (iii) HIPAA privacy/security rules (45 CFR Parts 160 and 164) for protected health information (PHI) handled by covered entities/business associates; and (iv) data integrity and electronic records controls (21 CFR Part 11) when electronic systems create/maintain FDA-required records. [2]
At the California level, the binding “overlay” most likely to affect oncology trials using AI/robotics includes: (i) California’s Human Experimentation statute (Health & Safety Code Chapter 1.3, §§ 24170–24179.5), including the Experimental Subject’s Bill of Rights requirement for “medical experiments”; (ii) California health data privacy rules under the Confidentiality of Medical Information Act (CMIA, Civil Code §§ 56 et seq.), plus California breach and facility reporting rules (Civil Code § 1798.82; Health & Safety Code § 1280.15 and implementing regulations); (iii) California’s consumer privacy law (CCPA/CPRA, Civil Code §§ 1798.100 et seq.)—often with a key practical caveat that clinical trial/biomedical research data may be statutorily exempt in many common trial contexts; and (iv) California medical practice laws relevant to AI systems that could be construed as diagnosing/treating or holding out as licensed professionals. [3]
Items with distinct oncology relevance include (a) FDA’s position that robotically-assisted surgical (RAS) systems have not received marketing authorization specifically for prevention/treatment of cancer and that oncology-related endpoints (e.g., recurrence, disease-free survival, overall survival) matter for trials of cancer-related robotic procedures; (b) oncology companion diagnostics and IVD co-development, which often makes AI-based imaging/pathology/genomic algorithms “medical devices” central to patient selection and thus subject to device/IDE and labeling-linked requirements; and (c) AI-driven analysis of medical images—explicitly a boundary condition for certain “non-device” clinical decision support exclusions.[4]
This report is an analytical synthesis of publicly available primary legal materials and official guidance as of March 24, 2026, and is not legal advice. [5]
Regulatory map for AI and robotics in oncology clinical trials
A workable way to determine governing requirements is to classify the AI/robotic component along two axes: (i) whether it is a regulated medical product (drug/biologic/device/combination) or a research operational tool; and (ii) whether it is investigational (new or new intended use) and “significant risk” (for devices). Device classification is anchored in the federal “device” definition (21 U.S.C. § 321(h)) and the device investigational exemption authority (21 U.S.C. § 360j(g)), implemented by 21 CFR Part 812. [6]
When the AI is “software intended for a medical purpose,” it may be regulated either as device software (including software as a medical device, SaMD) or fall under a statutory exclusion for certain software functions under FD&C Act § 520(o) (added by the 21st Century Cures Act), with FDA’s interpretations provided in software and clinical decision support guidance. In oncology, many AI models analyze medical images (radiology, pathology) and therefore frequently fall outside “non-device CDS” scenarios described in FDA’s CDS guidance. [7]
When robotics is used in an oncology trial as part of the intervention (e.g., robotic-assisted mastectomy or other cancer-related procedures), FDA has emphasized that cancer-related long-term outcomes may be necessary and that significant-risk investigations require IDE oversight when studying new intended uses. [8]
