Case law, enforcement examples, and identified gaps or ambiguities
Selected case law with practical relevance to AI/robotics clinical trials
California and federal courts have not yet produced a large, AI-oncology-trial-specific body of case law, but several widely cited decisions shape the liability and compliance perimeter for clinical research and regulated medical products.
In Moore v. Regents of the University of California[88](1990), the California Supreme Court recognized liability theories tied to failures of disclosure/informed consent regarding physicians’ research and economic interests while rejecting a broad property claim in excised cells. For oncology trials, the continuing relevance is that informed consent and fiduciary-like disclosure obligations can be central state-law risk drivers when research interests (including data monetization for AI training) are not properly disclosed. [89]
At the federal level, Riegel v. Medtronic (2008) is a key U.S. Supreme Court decision on medical device preemption for PMA-approved devices, and Buckman Co. v. Plaintiffs’ Legal Committee (2001) addresses implied preemption of “fraud-on-the-FDA” claims. While not trial-specific or AI-specific, these doctrines influence postmarket liability exposure for AI/robotics devices once approved and can shape litigation strategies after oncology device trials. [90]
Selected official enforcement and safety communications illustrating AI/robotics trial risk
FDA’s warning letter to Exer Labs, Inc.[60] illustrates that AI-enabled diagnostic claims can trigger device regulation and that FDA may cite lack of PMA or IDE authorization when a firm markets an AI diagnostic device without appropriate pathways. This is directly relevant to oncology trial ecosystems where AI tools are used for screening/diagnosis and may drift into promotional claims outside an authorized investigational context. [91]
For oncology-related robotics, FDA’s public materials on computer-assisted surgical systems emphasize that FDA has not granted marketing authorization for any robotically-assisted surgical device system specifically for prevention or treatment of cancer and highlight the importance of long-term cancer outcomes (recurrence, disease-free survival, overall survival) and IDE oversight for cancer-related robotic studies. This creates a distinct oncology-trial compliance and endpoint-design signal for robotics sponsors and IRBs. [8]
Gaps and ambiguities that commonly arise for AI/robotics in oncology trials
A recurring legal ambiguity is the boundary between “device” AI (regulated) and non-device trial software (not regulated as a device but still subject to GCP/privacy/data integrity). FDA’s updated CDS guidance and Cures Act interpretations help, but oncology AI often analyzes medical images or in vitro diagnostic signals, which tends to push it into regulated-device territory—yet hybrid systems (device + “other functions”) can blur responsibility and documentation boundaries. [92]
Adaptive AI updates during a trial remain operationally challenging: while PCCP concepts and AI lifecycle guidances support planned modifications, trials must still ensure protocol integrity, endpoint consistency, and auditability when an algorithm version changes mid-study (especially for imaging-based endpoints common in oncology). In practice, sponsors often need a governance approach that ties model versioning to protocol amendments, IDE supplements (if applicable), and Part 11 audit trails.[93]
Privacy law applicability can be uncertain across mixed data environments: CCPA/CPRA research exemptions can shield many regulated trial datasets, but trial recruitment and patient engagement layers (apps, chatbots, patient communities) may sit outside those exemptions, leading to parallel compliance duties under California consumer privacy law and/or HIPAA depending on entity roles and data types. [94]
Finally, “physical AI” robotics can introduce cross-cutting safety and professional practice issues not fully harmonized in a single framework: device regulation focuses on safety/effectiveness and investigational controls, while state medical practice rules focus on licensed practice and “holding out.” As robotics becomes more autonomous or AI-driven (e.g., autonomy features, AI-based “safety” layers), allocating responsibility between manufacturer, sponsor, investigator, surgeon, hospital credentialing, and software vendors can become legally and contractually complex. [95]
